# The agreement half of the adjacency ruling, for the containers where both spellings are
# malformed and so cannot be proven as a value. *A newline separates exactly like a space*, so the
# newline and spaced spellings of each source below must produce the SAME diagnostics — the point
# being that they are malformed in the same way, not that they are malformed at all.
#
# Its execution twin, `core-language/expressions/a-newline-separates-like-a-space`, runs the same
# shapes in the container where both spellings are well-formed. Both fixtures are regression guards,
# not witnesses: neither could have detected this fix, because the container that exhibited the
# defect differed only in its AST, and a fixture sees events and diagnostics, never a tree.
#
# The second group is the round-2 review finding on `spec/grammar.md:314`: a call still owed an
# operand is unfinished exactly as an operator is, so *"the unfinished value's own grammar position
# wins and no entry opens"*. `{ a: sentence 1 mod: 2 }` must be a malformed entry — not an entry
# keyed `mod` leaving `sentence` one argument short — and its newline spelling must agree.
:nums = [ 10 20 30 ]

# Adjacency: a parenthesized group. Both spellings are malformed, identically.
print ( :nums
[1] )
print ( :nums [1] )

# The regression this direction had to avoid, and the half its execution twin cannot carry: a
# newline after a comprehension or control header selects the long `... end` body form
# (`spec/grammar.md:34`), so the `[` binds as a BODY, not as a selector on the header's last
# operand. A narrowing can only make the `[` LESS likely to bind, so these are strictly more
# protected than before — asserted here rather than assumed. Each raises `ol-missing-end`.
print map n in :nums
[ :n ]

print filter n in :nums
[ :n ]

repeat 2
[ print 1 ]

# The dict entry lookahead must not fire while the value is owed an operand. `sentence` takes two
# inputs and has one, so no entry opens and the entry is malformed — as the spec's own
# `{ a: 1 + b: 2 }` example is.
print { a: sentence 1 mod: 2 }
print { a: sentence 1
mod: 2 }
print { a: sentence 1 and: 2 }
print { a: sentence 1
and: 2 }
